{
 "chapter": "gceal-maths-differentiation",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "id": "gceal-maths-diff-product-quotient",
   "title": "Product and quotient rules",
   "minutes": 25,
   "programRef": "Cameroon GCE Board — Advanced Level Mathematics, Pure Mathematics (Upper Sixth part; to be checked against the official syllabus)",
   "status": "draft",
   "author": "CastBridge (AI draft)",
   "source": "Original writing following the Cameroonian curriculum; to be reviewed by a teacher",
   "objectives": [
    "Differentiate products with the product rule",
    "Differentiate quotients with the quotient rule",
    "Find stationary points of such functions"
   ],
   "prerequisites": [],
   "exercises": [
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e01",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e02",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e03",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e04",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e05",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e06"
   ],
   "selfCheck": [
    "gceal-maths-diff-product-quotient-q1",
    "gceal-maths-diff-product-quotient-q2",
    "gceal-maths-diff-product-quotient-q3",
    "gceal-maths-diff-product-quotient-q4",
    "gceal-maths-diff-product-quotient-q5"
   ],
   "reviewNotes": [
    "Syllabus references, scope and level of difficulty are to be checked against the official text; all facts, formulas and worked examples to be verified by a teacher before release."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Product rule",
     "md": "If $y=uv$ then $\\frac{dy}{dx}=u\\frac{dv}{dx}+v\\frac{du}{dx}$. Use it when two functions of $x$ are multiplied, for example $x^2e^{3x}$, $x\\ln x$, $x\\sin x$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Quotient rule",
     "md": "If $y=\\frac{u}{v}$ then $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{v\\frac{du}{dx}-u\\frac{dv}{dx}}{v^2}$. The order in the numerator matters: bottom times derivative of top, minus top times derivative of bottom."
    },
    {
     "type": "illustration",
     "figure": {
      "kind": "plot",
      "xmin": 0,
      "xmax": 5,
      "ymin": -0.1,
      "ymax": 0.5,
      "grid": 1,
      "w": 400,
      "h": 260,
      "curves": [
       {
        "expr": "x*exp(-x)",
        "color": "blue"
       }
      ],
      "points": [
       {
        "x": 1,
        "y": 0.3679,
        "label": "max (1, 0.37)",
        "color": "red"
       }
      ],
      "segments": [],
      "xlabel": "x"
     },
     "caption": "The curve $y=xe^{-x}$ has its maximum at $(1,e^{-1})$, found with the product rule.",
     "alt": "A curve rising from the origin to a peak at x=1 then slowly decreasing to 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Method",
     "md": "1. Name $u$ and $v$ and differentiate each separately. 2. Substitute into the formula. 3. Factorise the result (common factors such as $e^{x}$) so that setting $\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$ is easy. Check with a number if possible."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- $\\frac{d}{dx}(uv)\\neq\\frac{du}{dx}\\times\\frac{dv}{dx}$.\n- Reversing the numerator of the quotient rule gives the wrong sign.\n- Forgetting that $e^x$ is never zero when solving $\\frac{dy}{dx}=0$."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Differentiate $y=x^2e^{3x}$.",
     "steps": [
      "Let $u=x^2$, $v=e^{3x}$: $u'=2x$, $v'=3e^{3x}$.",
      "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=x^2\\cdot3e^{3x}+e^{3x}\\cdot2x=xe^{3x}(3x+2)$."
     ],
     "answer": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=xe^{3x}(3x+2)$"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "Find the gradient of $y=\\frac{2x+1}{x^2+1}$ at $x=1$.",
     "steps": [
      "$u=2x+1$, $v=x^2+1$: $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(x^2+1)(2)-(2x+1)(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2}=\\frac{-2x^2-2x+2}{(x^2+1)^2}$.",
      "At $x=1$: $\\frac{-2-2+2}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}$."
     ],
     "answer": "Gradient $-\\frac{1}{2}$"
    }
   ],
