   "id": "gceal-maths-diff-chain-trig",
   "title": "Chain rule and trigonometric functions",
   "minutes": 25,
   "programRef": "Cameroon GCE Board — Advanced Level Mathematics, Pure Mathematics (Upper Sixth part; to be checked against the official syllabus)",
   "status": "draft",
   "author": "CastBridge (AI draft)",
   "source": "Original writing following the Cameroonian curriculum; to be reviewed by a teacher",
   "objectives": [
    "Use the chain rule",
    "Differentiate $\\sin$, $\\cos$ and $\\tan$ (x in radians)",
    "Combine the chain, product and quotient rules"
   ],
   "prerequisites": [],
   "exercises": [
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e07",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e08",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e09",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e10",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e11",
    "gceal-maths-differentiation-e12"
   ],
   "selfCheck": [
    "gceal-maths-diff-chain-trig-q1",
    "gceal-maths-diff-chain-trig-q2",
    "gceal-maths-diff-chain-trig-q3",
    "gceal-maths-diff-chain-trig-q4",
    "gceal-maths-diff-chain-trig-q5"
   ],
   "reviewNotes": [
    "Derivatives of sec, cosec, cot, inverse trig functions not covered; check the syllabus.",
    "Syllabus references, scope and level of difficulty are to be checked against the official text; all facts, formulas and worked examples to be verified by a teacher before release."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Chain rule",
     "md": "If $y=f(u)$ and $u=g(x)$ then $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy}{du}\\times\\frac{du}{dx}$. In practice: differentiate the outer function, keep the inside, then multiply by the derivative of the inside. E.g. $\\frac{d}{dx}(3x^2+1)^5=5(3x^2+1)^4\\times6x$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Trigonometric derivatives (radians)",
     "md": "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin x=\\cos x$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos x=-\\sin x$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\tan x=\\frac{1}{\\cos^2x}$. With a constant: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin kx=k\\cos kx$, $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos kx=-k\\sin kx$. Also $\\frac{d}{dx}e^{f(x)}=f'(x)e^{f(x)}$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln f(x)=\\frac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "illustration",
     "figure": {
      "kind": "plot",
      "xmin": 0,
      "xmax": 6.4,
      "ymin": -1.3,
      "ymax": 1.3,
      "grid": 1,
      "w": 400,
      "h": 260,
      "curves": [
       {
        "expr": "sin(x)",
        "color": "blue",
        "label": "sin x"
       },
       {
        "expr": "cos(x)",
        "color": "orange",
        "label": "cos x"
       }
      ],
      "points": [],
      "segments": [],
      "xlabel": "x (radians)"
     },
     "caption": "$y=\\sin x$ (blue) and its gradient function $y=\\cos x$ (orange): where $\\sin x$ peaks, $\\cos x=0$.",
     "alt": "Sine and cosine curves over one period, cosine is the gradient of sine."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Forgetting the derivative of the inside: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\sin x^2=2x\\cos x^2$, not $\\cos x^2$.\n- These formulae are only valid when $x$ is in RADIANS.\n- $\\frac{d}{dx}\\cos x$ has a minus sign."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Differentiate $y=(3x^2+1)^5$.",
     "steps": [
      "Outer: $u^5$ gives $5u^4$. Inner: $u=3x^2+1$ gives $6x$.",
      "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=5(3x^2+1)^4\\times6x=30x(3x^2+1)^4$."
     ],
     "answer": "$30x(3x^2+1)^4$"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "Differentiate $y=e^x\\sin2x$.",
     "steps": [
      "Product rule with $u=e^x$, $v=\\sin2x$ ($v'=2\\cos2x$ by the chain rule).",
      "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=e^x\\cdot2\\cos2x+\\sin2x\\cdot e^x=e^x(\\sin2x+2\\cos2x)$."
     ],
     "answer": "$e^x(\\sin2x+2\\cos2x)$"
    }
   ],
